  "ideasTulipFieldTitle": "Tulip field",
  "ideasTulipFieldSummary": "Rows of red and yellow tulips running to the horizon, spring green between them.",
  "ideasTulipFieldPractice": "Flat bands that break into dots as they come closer.",
  "ideasTulipFieldSubject": "Rows of tulips seen from low down, with a low horizon.",
  "ideasTulipFieldTip": "The nearest row is dots, the furthest is a stripe: change the mark as the row goes back.",
  "ideasTulipFieldStep1": "Pencil rows that narrow towards a low horizon.",
  "ideasTulipFieldStep2": "Red in the first rows: single blobs near, a solid stripe further back.",
  "ideasTulipFieldStep3": "Yellow in the next rows, the same way.",
  "ideasTulipFieldStep4": "Light green between the rows, and along the horizon.",
  "ideasTulipFieldStep5": "Darker green in a thin line at the foot of each row.",
  "ideasSummerBeachTitle": "At the beach",
  "ideasSummerBeachSummary": "Sand, a strip of sea, a parasol: three flat bands and one bright accent.",
  "ideasSummerBeachPractice": "Three flat bands and a single accent that carries the eye.",
  "ideasSummerBeachSubject": "A beach seen from the sand, with a parasol.",
  "ideasSummerBeachTip": "Leave the foam as paper: it is the brightest line in the sea.",
  "ideasSummerBeachStep1": "Pencil three horizontal bands (sky, sea, sand) and a parasol in the sand.",
  "ideasSummerBeachStep2": "Pale sand with a very light hand; leave the wet sand near the water lighter.",
  "ideasSummerBeachStep3": "Light teal for the sea, with a line of paper for the foam.",
  "ideasSummerBeachStep4": "Deeper teal at the horizon.",
  "ideasSummerBeachStep5": "Red parasol and its small shadow on the sand, painted with a second layer of the sand colour.",
  "ideasWatermelonSliceTitle": "Watermelon",
  "ideasWatermelonSliceSummary": "A slice of watermelon: red flesh, white rind, green skin, black seeds.",
  "ideasWatermelonSlicePractice": "A quick blend and a flat stripe, with the white rind left as paper.",
  "ideasWatermelonSliceSubject": "A triangular slice of watermelon with a curved base.",
  "ideasWatermelonSliceTip": "The white rind is paper: do not colour it at all.",
  "ideasWatermelonSliceStep1": "Pencil a triangle with a curved base and a thin white band inside the green.",
  "ideasWatermelonSliceStep2": "The light red over the flesh, paler near the rind.",
  "ideasWatermelonSliceStep3": "Deeper red towards the point, blended while wet.",
  "ideasWatermelonSliceStep4": "Green on the skin, with dark green stripes in a second layer.",
  "ideasWatermelonSliceStep5": "Seeds with the liner, each a small teardrop.",
  "ideasForestMushroomsTitle": "Mushrooms on the forest floor",
  "ideasForestMushroomsSummary": "A cluster of mushrooms among brown leaves in autumn.",
  "ideasForestMushroomsPractice": "Round caps with small blends and a loose, dry ground.",
  "ideasForestMushroomsSubject": "Three mushrooms of different heights among fallen leaves.",
  "ideasForestMushroomsTip": "The forest floor wants loose, broken strokes of brown, not solid fills.",
  "ideasForestMushroomsStep1": "Pencil three mushrooms of different heights and a few leaves around them.",
  "ideasForestMushroomsStep2": "Pale beige on the stems and the underside of the caps.",
  "ideasForestMushroomsStep3": "The red-brown on the caps, darker at the edge.",
  "ideasForestMushroomsStep4": "Brown on the leaves and the ground.",
  "ideasForestMushroomsStep5": "Darkest under the caps and where the stems meet the ground; white gel spots on the caps if you like.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketTitle": "Harvest basket",
  "ideasHarvestBasketSummary": "Apples, a pear and a small squash in a basket, each a different colour, shaded with one grey.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketPractice": "One grey shading three strong colours so they belong together.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketSubject": "A basket full of fruit: apples, a pear and a squash.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketTip": "Shade every fruit with the same grey. It is what keeps a busy still life together.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketStep1": "Pencil the basket and the fruit overlapping.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketStep2": "Red for the apples, with the highlight left as paper.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketStep3": "Yellow-green for the pear.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketStep4": "Orange for the squash, with its ridges as darker lines of a second layer.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketStep5": "The same grey for every shadow and the inside of the basket.",
  "ideasHarvestBasketStep6": "The darkest marker for the weave lines of the basket.",
  "ideasHotChocolateTitle": "Hot chocolate",
  "ideasHotChocolateSummary": "A mug of chocolate with steam, a marshmallow and a red mitten.",
  "ideasHotChocolatePractice": "Warm browns, one red accent and steam lifted out of the background.",
  "ideasHotChocolateSubject": "A mug of hot chocolate with a marshmallow and a mitten beside it.",
  "ideasHotChocolateTip": "Steam is either paper or a shape lifted with the blender, never a drawn line.",
  "ideasHotChocolateStep1": "Pencil the mug, the surface of the drink, a marshmallow and a mitten resting against it.",
  "ideasHotChocolateStep2": "Pale brown on the lit side of the mug and on the shadow of the marshmallow.",
  "ideasHotChocolateStep3": "Brown on the surface of the drink and the shadow side of the mug.",
  "ideasHotChocolateStep4": "Red mitten, two layers.",
  "ideasHotChocolateStep5": "Steam: lift wisps out of the background with the blender, or leave them as paper.",
  "ideasLunarNewYearTitle": "Lunar New Year",
  "ideasLunarNewYearSummary": "Red lanterns with gold trim against a red wall.",
  "ideasLunarNewYearPractice": "One red family in three steps and a flat gold accent.",
  "ideasLunarNewYearSubject": "Three round lanterns hanging at different heights, with tassels.",
  "ideasLunarNewYearTip": "Lay the gold on top of the dry red, never the other way round.",
  "ideasLunarNewYearStep1": "Pencil three lanterns hanging at different heights, with tassels.",
  "ideasLunarNewYearStep2": "Light red over the lanterns, paler on the lit side.",
  "ideasLunarNewYearStep3": "The red on the shadow side and the ribs, blended while wet.",
  "ideasLunarNewYearStep4": "Dark red between the ribs and inside the rims.",
  "ideasLunarNewYearStep5": "Gold yellow on the rims, the caps and the tassels, left crisp.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsTitle": "Diwali lamps",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsSummary": "A row of clay lamps with small flames and a rangoli of bright colours around them.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsPractice": "A flat saturated pattern next to a two-step blend on the lamps.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsSubject": "Five little clay lamps in a curve, with a simple symmetrical pattern around them.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsTip": "Keep the pattern flat and hard-edged. It is the contrast with the lamps that makes it work.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsStep1": "Pencil five little lamps in a curve and a simple symmetrical pattern around them.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsStep2": "Light terracotta on the lamps.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsStep3": "Deeper terracotta on their shadow side.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsStep4": "Yellow for each flame and a glow on the rim of each lamp.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsStep5": "Magenta in the alternating sections of the pattern, flat and hard-edged.",
  "ideasDiwaliLampsStep6": "Teal in the other sections, then white gel dots on the flames if you like.",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesTitle": "Hanukkah",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesSummary": "A menorah with its candles lit, blue and white with gold flames.",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesPractice": "White shapes kept as paper against a blue field.",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesSubject": "A menorah with its candles lit in front of a blue background.",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesTip": "The candles are paper: colour around them and they come out white.",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesStep1": "Pencil the menorah and the candles; the candles stay paper.",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesStep2": "Light blue background around everything.",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesStep3": "Deeper blue at the edges of the page, blended inwards.",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesStep4": "The dark blue for the menorah itself.",
  "ideasHanukkahCandlesStep5": "Yellow flames and a soft glow around each, then a white gel core in each flame.",
  "ideasPaintedEggsTitle": "Painted eggs",
  "ideasPaintedEggsSummary": "Three decorated eggs with stripes, dots and zigzags in mid-strength colours.",
  "ideasPaintedEggsPractice": "Three ways to make a pattern: paper, shape and second layer.",
  "ideasPaintedEggsSubject": "Three eggs, each with a different pattern.",
  "ideasPaintedEggsTip": "Mid-strength colours let a second layer show without turning muddy.",
  "ideasPaintedEggsStep1": "Pencil three eggs and a different pattern on each.",
  "ideasPaintedEggsStep2": "First egg: base colour flat, with the pattern left as paper.",
  "ideasPaintedEggsStep3": "Second egg: fill the pattern shapes only, the rest stays paper.",
  "ideasPaintedEggsStep4": "Third egg: colour it, then add the pattern in the same colour as a second layer.",
  "ideasPaintedEggsStep5": "One grey for the shadow side of each egg and the small shadow under it.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosTitle": "Día de Muertos",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosSummary": "Marigolds and a decorated skull in orange, magenta and teal on a dark ground.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosPractice": "Saturated flat pattern on a dark field.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosSubject": "A decorated skull with flower shapes round the eyes and a bank of marigolds below.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosTip": "Lay the dark ground last and slowly: dark ink runs into the light areas.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosStep1": "Pencil a skull with flower shapes round the eyes and a bank of marigolds below.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosStep2": "Light orange on the marigolds, each as a rough circle.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosStep3": "Deeper orange in short strokes inside each circle for the petals.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosStep4": "Magenta for the patterns of the skull, flat and symmetrical.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosStep5": "Teal for the other half of the patterns of the skull.",
  "ideasDiaDeMuertosStep6": "The darkest marker for the eye sockets and the background, then liner details.",
  "ideasValentineCardTitle": "Valentine card",
  "ideasValentineCardSummary": "A card with three hearts, from pale pink to deep red, and a shadow line that lifts them off the paper.",
  "ideasValentineCardPractice": "Three flat fills and a drop-shadow line.",
  "ideasValentineCardSubject": "Three overlapping hearts, the biggest at the back.",
  "ideasValentineCardTip": "Draw the drop shadow as one thin line: it lifts the heart without more colour.",
  "ideasValentineCardStep1": "Pencil three overlapping hearts, the biggest at the back.",
  "ideasValentineCardStep2": "Pale pink on the back heart.",
  "ideasValentineCardStep3": "Pink on the middle one.",
  "ideasValentineCardStep4": "Red on the front one, and a thin line of it under the other two as a drop shadow.",
  "ideasValentineCardStep5": "A white gel highlight on each heart, top left.",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskTitle": "Carnival mask",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskSummary": "A mask with feathers in violet, teal and gold.",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskPractice": "Flicks for feathers, a blend for the mask and flat gold.",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskSubject": "A half mask with three feathers rising from one side.",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskTip": "Flick the feathers outwards from the quill: the soft edge is what makes them read as feathers.",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskStep1": "Pencil a half mask and three feathers rising from one side.",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskStep2": "Violet on the mask, two layers where it curves away.",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskStep3": "Teal on the feathers, flicked outwards from the quill so the edges are soft.",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskStep4": "Gold on the edge of the mask and the eye rims.",
  "ideasCarnivalMaskStep5": "Darkest inside the eye holes, then gel dots as sequins.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownTitle": "Midsummer flower crown",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownSummary": "A crown of wildflowers and leaves: blue, yellow and pink on green.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownPractice": "Many small dabs of the fine nib on a dry green base.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownSubject": "A ring of leaves with three kinds of small wildflowers.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownTip": "Dabs of colour on a dry base stay crisp. Wait for the green to dry before you start the flowers.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownStep1": "Pencil a ring of leaves and small flowers in three kinds.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownStep2": "Light green on all the leaves.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownStep3": "Darker green where leaves overlap and underneath.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownStep4": "Blue on the first kind of flower.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownStep5": "Yellow on the second kind of flower.",
  "ideasMidsummerCrownStep6": "Pink on the third, with a tiny second layer at the centre of each flower.",
  "ideasEidNightTitle": "Eid night",
  "ideasEidNightSummary": "A crescent moon and a hanging lantern against a deep teal and violet sky.",
  "ideasEidNightPractice": "A clean wet blend from teal to violet, with gold laid on top.",
  "ideasEidNightSubject": "A crescent moon and a patterned lantern against a night sky.",
  "ideasEidNightTip": "Blend the teal into the violet while both are wet. Once dry, the sky stays clean.",
  "ideasEidNightStep1": "Pencil the crescent and a patterned lantern; both stay paper for now.",
  "ideasEidNightStep2": "Teal across the sky, lightest near the moon.",
  "ideasEidNightStep3": "Deeper teal towards the edges.",
  "ideasEidNightStep4": "Violet blended into the top corners while the teal is still damp.",
  "ideasEidNightStep5": "Gold on the crescent and the lantern frame; the panes stay pale, then white gel stars.",
  "ideasHoliColoursTitle": "Holi",
  "ideasHoliColoursSummary": "Clouds of coloured powder on white: soft edges, hard centres, four bright colours.",
  "ideasHoliColoursPractice": "Colour with no edge: the blender dissolves an edge into paper.",
  "ideasHoliColoursSubject": "Four overlapping clouds of coloured powder on white paper.",
  "ideasHoliColoursTip": "Let each cloud dry before you add the next one, or the colours all turn to mud.",
  "ideasHoliColoursStep1": "Scribble a loose cloud with the first colour, dense in the middle.",
  "ideasHoliColoursStep2": "Pull its edges out with the blender into the white paper until they vanish.",
  "ideasHoliColoursStep3": "A second cloud overlapping the first a little; where they meet, let them mix.",
  "ideasHoliColoursStep4": "A third cloud, smaller, in a gap.",
  "ideasHoliColoursStep5": "A fourth cloud in the last gap, then flick a few dots of each colour around it.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamTitle": "Ice cream",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamSummary": "Three scoops of different flavours on a cone.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamPractice": "Three round blends and one grey shading all of them.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamSubject": "Three stacked scoops on a cone with a waffle grid.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamTip": "Use the same grey for every scoop: that is what keeps three different colours together.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamStep1": "Pencil a cone with a waffle grid and three stacked scoops.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamStep2": "The first flavour on the bottom scoop, with a highlight of paper left on it.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamStep3": "The second flavour on the middle scoop, with its own highlight.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamStep4": "The third flavour on the top scoop.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamStep5": "The same light grey on the underside of every scoop and where they overlap.",
  "ideasSummerIceCreamStep6": "The cone in beige, with the grid lines in a second layer.",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksTitle": "New Year fireworks",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksSummary": "Three bursts of fireworks over a dark sky, each a different colour.",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksPractice": "Bright colour over a dark fill, with the lines kept as paper.",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksSubject": "Three bursts of fireworks with light lines radiating from each centre.",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksTip": "Fill the sky around the lines, not over them: the white paper is what makes the fireworks glow.",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksStep1": "Pencil three centres and light radiating lines from each; the lines stay paper for now.",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksStep2": "Fill the sky dark, going round the lines with the fine nib (they can be uneven).",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksStep3": "Trace the lines of the first burst with the first colour, pressing harder at the tips.",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksStep4": "The lines of the second burst in the second colour.",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksStep5": "The lines of the third burst in the third colour.",
  "ideasNewYearFireworksStep6": "White gel at the centre of each burst and a few sparks at the tips.",
  "ideasCozyLampTitle": "Cosy corner",
  "ideasCozyLampSummary": "An armchair under a lamp in the evening: a warm pool of light and soft brown shadows.",
  "ideasCozyLampPractice": "Planning a lit area and letting the rest of the room fall into warm brown.",
  "ideasCozyLampSubject": "An armchair, a floor lamp and a wall behind them.",
  "ideasCozyLampTip": "Keep the darkest brown out of the circle of light: the lamp reaches no corner.",
  "ideasCozyLampStep1": "Pencil the chair, the lamp and the circle of light it throws on the floor and the wall.",
  "ideasCozyLampStep2": "Fill the circle of light with the pale yellow and fade it out with the colourless blender.",
  "ideasCozyLampStep3": "Colour the rest of the room with the light warm beige, flat and even.",
  "ideasCozyLampStep4": "Put the warm brown on the chair and on the side of the room that is away from the lamp.",
  "ideasCozyLampStep5": "Use the dark brown in the corners and under the chair, and keep it off the circle of light.",
  "ideasFoggyMorningTitle": "Fog",
  "ideasFoggyMorningSummary": "Trees disappearing into fog: the nearer ones darker, the further ones paler.",
  "ideasFoggyMorningPractice": "Depth with a pale glaze over dark shapes.",
  "ideasFoggyMorningSubject": "Three rows of trees on a road, each row further back.",
  "ideasFoggyMorningTip": "A pale glaze over a dark shape is the simplest fog there is: the ink under it stays.",
  "ideasFoggyMorningStep1": "Pencil three rows of trees, each row further back, and mark where the ground starts.",
  "ideasFoggyMorningStep2": "Colour the furthest row with the palest marker only, without any detail.",
  "ideasFoggyMorningStep3": "Colour the middle row with the base tone and soften its lower edge into the ground with the blender.",
  "ideasFoggyMorningStep4": "Draw the nearest trees with the dark marker and add a few branches.",
  "ideasFoggyMorningStep5": "Glaze the pale marker over the top of every row, so the fog lies over the whole scene.",
  "ideasRainyWindowTitle": "Rain on the window",
  "ideasRainyWindowSummary": "Blurred lights seen through a wet window, with drops running down the glass.",
  "ideasRainyWindowPractice": "Glowing lights behind glass, and crisp marks on top of them.",
  "ideasRainyWindowSubject": "A window at night with street lights outside and three drops on the glass.",
  "ideasRainyWindowTip": "The lights must dry completely before the blue goes over them, or the colours mix.",
  "ideasRainyWindowStep1": "Pencil the window frame and a few blurred blobs of light outside, and mark the path of three drops.",
  "ideasRainyWindowStep2": "Colour the warm lights as soft blobs and melt their edges with the blender.",
  "ideasRainyWindowStep3": "Cover the whole glass with the light blue, working around the lights.",
  "ideasRainyWindowStep4": "Add the base blue towards the corners and leave the drop paths a little lighter.",
  "ideasRainyWindowStep5": "Colour the frame dark and put a dark crescent on the side of each drop away from the light.",
  "ideasRainyWindowStep6": "Once dry, add a touch of white gel on the lit side of each drop.",
  "ideasEerieForestTitle": "Eerie woods",
  "ideasEerieForestSummary": "Thin trees at dusk in sickly greens, with a violet sky between them.",
  "ideasEerieForestPractice": "Letting two colours mix where they meet, on purpose.",
  "ideasEerieForestSubject": "Tall thin trunks on a low horizon at dusk.",
  "ideasEerieForestTip": "The green and the violet turn grey where they meet. That grey is the mood, not a mistake.",
  "ideasEerieForestStep1": "Pencil tall thin trunks and a low horizon.",
  "ideasEerieForestStep2": "Put a pale yellow-green along the horizon, between the trunks.",
  "ideasEerieForestStep3": "Colour the violet above it and blend it into the green while it is damp.",
  "ideasEerieForestStep4": "Colour the green on the ground and on the lower part of the trunks.",
  "ideasEerieForestStep5": "Use the darkest green on the trunks in uneven strokes, and add long shadows across the ground.",
  "ideasGoldenHourTitle": "Golden hour",
  "ideasGoldenHourSummary": "A white wall and a doorway at the end of the afternoon: orange light, long violet shadows.",
  "ideasGoldenHourPractice": "One warm glaze over everything, with hard-edged cool shadows on top.",
  "ideasGoldenHourSubject": "A wall with a door, a plant and the long shadow the plant throws on the wall.",
  "ideasGoldenHourTip": "The wall is not white here: in this light it is orange. Paint it so.",
  "ideasGoldenHourStep1": "Pencil a wall with a door and a plant, and the long shadow the plant throws across the wall.",
  "ideasGoldenHourStep2": "Wash the whole wall with the pale orange: that is the white wall in this light.",
  "ideasGoldenHourStep3": "Put the deeper orange under the lintel and on the door.",
  "ideasGoldenHourStep4": "Add the violet-grey in the shadow of the plant and in the doorway, with a crisp edge.",
  "ideasGoldenHourStep5": "Keep the darkest marker for the depth of the doorway only.",
  "ideasStormySeaTitle": "Stormy sea",
  "ideasStormySeaSummary": "Grey-green waves under a dark sky, with white paper for the foam.",
  "ideasStormySeaPractice": "Pale flat water against a heavy sky, with the foam left as paper.",
  "ideasStormySeaSubject": "A low horizon, two big waves and the foam breaking on them.",
  "ideasStormySeaTip": "The foam is paper you did not touch: plan where it breaks before you colour the water.",
  "ideasStormySeaStep1": "Pencil the horizon low, two big waves and where the foam breaks. The foam stays paper.",
  "ideasStormySeaStep2": "Cover the water with the pale grey-green, going round the foam.",
  "ideasStormySeaStep3": "Put the darker water in the troughs and under each crest.",
  "ideasStormySeaStep4": "Fill the sky with the dark marker, darkest at the top, and the deepest water under the breaking wave.",
  "ideasStormySeaStep5": "Once dry, add white gel for the spray flicked off the crests: a few dots only.",
  "ideasCandleFlameTitle": "By candlelight",
  "ideasCandleFlameSummary": "A candle on a table in a dark room: everything warm, and nothing far from the flame visible.",
  "ideasCandleFlamePractice": "Concentric rings of warm colour, blended wet into each other.",
  "ideasCandleFlameSubject": "A candle in a holder, with the table and the far wall behind it.",
  "ideasCandleFlameTip": "Only what the flame touches is visible: the far room can be almost one dark shape.",
  "ideasCandleFlameStep1": "Pencil the candle, its flame and a circle of light on the table. The core of the flame stays paper.",
  "ideasCandleFlameStep2": "Colour the halo of the flame and the nearest part of the table with the yellow, melted outwards with the blender.",
  "ideasCandleFlameStep3": "Put the warm orange on the lit side of the candle and on the next ring of the table.",
  "ideasCandleFlameStep4": "Colour brown everything beyond the circle of light.",
  "ideasCandleFlameStep5": "Use the darkest brown for the far room and the shadow side of the candle, with a soft edge against the light.",
  "ideasDesertNoonTitle": "Desert noon",
  "ideasDesertNoonSummary": "Sand, one rock and a hard shadow under a bleached sky.",
  "ideasDesertNoonPractice": "A flat pale fill against one hard, dark shadow.",
  "ideasDesertNoonSubject": "A high horizon, a single rock and its short shadow straight under it.",
  "ideasDesertNoonTip": "Leave a band of white paper at the horizon: that band is what makes the sky look hot.",
  "ideasDesertNoonStep1": "Pencil a high horizon, one rock and its short shadow straight under it.",
  "ideasDesertNoonStep2": "Cover the sand below the horizon with the pale yellow, in one very light pass.",
  "ideasDesertNoonStep3": "Colour the sky with the pale blue and leave it paler near the horizon.",
  "ideasDesertNoonStep4": "Put the orange on the shadow side of the rock and on the ripples of the sand.",
  "ideasDesertNoonStep5": "Use the violet-blue only in the shadow the rock casts, with a hard edge.",
  "ideasBokehLightsTitle": "Blurred lights",
  "ideasBokehLightsSummary": "A background of soft out-of-focus circles of light.",
  "ideasBokehLightsPractice": "Making a soft disc with the blender on a flat field.",
  "ideasBokehLightsSubject": "A dark field with a dozen blurred lights of different sizes.",
  "ideasBokehLightsTip": "Press the blender flat: a pointed nib draws a line, not a disc.",
  "ideasBokehLightsStep1": "Fill the whole area with the base colour, in flat strokes.",
  "ideasBokehLightsStep2": "Darken the corners and blend the dark inwards while the ink is damp.",
  "ideasBokehLightsStep3": "Let it dry, then press the broad nib of the blender flat in circles of different sizes.",
  "ideasBokehLightsStep4": "Touch the light marker into the centre of a few of the discs so they glow.",
  "ideasBokehLightsStep5": "Put a white gel dot inside two or three of the discs, not in all of them.",
  "ideasRetroPosterTitle": "Retro poster",
  "ideasRetroPosterSummary": "Three flat, slightly dusty colours and one dark, with no blending at all.",
  "ideasRetroPosterPractice": "Flat fills and hard edges, the way a printed poster looks.",
  "ideasRetroPosterSubject": "A lighthouse, a car or a bottle drawn as a few big flat shapes.",
  "ideasRetroPosterTip": "Leave a hair of white paper between the colours, as if the print had slipped a little.",
  "ideasRetroPosterStep1": "Pencil a simple subject as big flat shapes: a lighthouse, a car, a bottle.",
  "ideasRetroPosterStep2": "Fill the biggest shapes flat, in one pass.",
  "ideasRetroPosterStep3": "Fill the next shapes and leave a hair of paper between the colours.",
  "ideasRetroPosterStep4": "Use the third colour on the smallest shapes.",
  "ideasRetroPosterStep5": "Draw the shadows as separate flat shapes with the dark marker.",
  "ideasRetroPosterStep6": "Add a few liner lines where the printed poster would have them.",
  "ideasFreshCitrusTitle": "Fresh and zesty",
  "ideasFreshCitrusSummary": "A lemon, a lime and a glass of water, with lots of white paper and at most two layers.",
  "ideasFreshCitrusPractice": "Bright colour on white, stopped early.",
  "ideasFreshCitrusSubject": "A lemon, a lime and a glass of water on a table.",
  "ideasFreshCitrusTip": "Stop after two layers: a third one takes the fresh out of the colour.",
  "ideasFreshCitrusStep1": "Pencil the fruit and the glass, and keep the composition airy.",
  "ideasFreshCitrusStep2": "Colour the lemon with the yellow in one pass, leaving a white highlight.",
  "ideasFreshCitrusStep3": "Colour the lime with the green, also in one pass.",
  "ideasFreshCitrusStep4": "The darker yellow on the shadow side of the lemon, and a second pass of green on the shadow side of the lime.",
  "ideasFreshCitrusStep5": "Use the pale blue for the water in the glass and a small shadow under each fruit.",
  "ideasTropicalNoonTitle": "Tropical noon",
  "ideasTropicalNoonSummary": "Big leaves, a hot pink flower and a slice of blue sky, saturated and hard-edged.",
  "ideasTropicalNoonPractice": "Strong flat colour with crisp overlaps, without any blending.",
  "ideasTropicalNoonSubject": "Two or three big leaves overlapping, one flower and the sky in the gaps.",
  "ideasTropicalNoonTip": "Leave the edge of each leaf sharp: the overlap is what sells the depth here.",
  "ideasTropicalNoonStep1": "Pencil two or three large leaves overlapping, one flower and the sky in the gaps between them.",
  "ideasTropicalNoonStep2": "Colour the leaves with the light green, following the veins.",
  "ideasTropicalNoonStep3": "Put the mid green on the leaves behind and under the others, with hard edges where they overlap.",
  "ideasTropicalNoonStep4": "Colour the hot pink on the flower, with two layers.",
  "ideasTropicalNoonStep5": "Colour the blue sky in the gaps, right up to the edge of the leaves.",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightTitle": "In the greenhouse",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightSummary": "Plants behind glass in damp green light, with pale grey panes.",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightPractice": "A pale glaze over a scene, the way glass veils it.",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightSubject": "A glass wall with its bars and pots of plants behind it.",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightTip": "The green glaze goes on last: it is the damp light of the place, not the colour of the plants.",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightStep1": "Pencil a glass wall with its bars and pots of plants behind it.",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightStep2": "Colour the plants with the base green, softer the further they are behind the glass.",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightStep3": "Keep the dark green for the nearest leaves only.",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightStep4": "Use the light grey for the bars and for the reflection streaks on the panes.",
  "ideasGreenhouseLightStep5": "Once dry, glaze the whole glass area with the pale green: the light inside is green.",
  "ideasDustyAtticTitle": "Dusty attic",
  "ideasDustyAtticSummary": "Warm greys, an old trunk and one shaft of light with dust in it.",
  "ideasDustyAtticPractice": "A grey room with one bright place left untouched.",
  "ideasDustyAtticSubject": "A small high window, the shaft of light it throws and an old trunk in it.",
  "ideasDustyAtticTip": "Keep the shaft of light the brightest thing in the picture: it is the only white paper left.",
  "ideasDustyAtticStep1": "Pencil a small window high up, the shaft of light it throws and a trunk inside it.",
  "ideasDustyAtticStep2": "Cover everything with the lightest warm grey, except the inside of the shaft.",
  "ideasDustyAtticStep3": "Put the mid warm grey on the walls away from the window and on the sides of the trunk.",
  "ideasDustyAtticStep4": "Use the darkest grey in the corners and under the trunk.",
  "ideasDustyAtticStep5": "Add a faint pale yellow glaze inside the shaft of light.",
  "ideasDustyAtticStep6": "Once dry, add a few white gel specks of dust in the shaft.",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackTitle": "Colour, grey and black",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackSummary": "Only three markers: one colour, one grey and one very dark.",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackPractice": "Placing the light with the fewest markers you have.",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackSubject": "A bike, a boot or a teapot with one clear colour.",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackTip": "With three nibs there is nothing to decide but where the light goes. Plan the light first.",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackStep1": "Pick a subject with one main colour and mark in pencil where the light comes from.",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackStep2": "Colour the whole subject with the colour, and give it two layers where it turns away from the light.",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackStep3": "Use the grey for the cast shadow and for anything that is not the subject.",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackStep4": "Keep the dark marker for the contact lines and the deepest corners.",
  "ideasColourGreyBlackStep5": "Step back: the drawing reads with just three values, and the grey holds them together.",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioTitle": "Three neighbours",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioSummary": "Three colours that sit next to each other on the wheel, and nothing from the other side.",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioPractice": "Building a scheme out of neighbouring hues.",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioSubject": "A sunflower, a sea or a field of one colour.",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioTip": "Neighbouring colours blend while wet without turning grey. Keep them wet and let them mix.",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioStep1": "Choose a subject that is naturally one colour, such as a sunflower or a sea, and draw it.",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioStep2": "Cover most of the subject with the middle colour of the three.",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioStep3": "Put the neighbour on the lit side and blend it into the middle colour while it is wet.",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioStep4": "Use the other neighbour in the shadows, away from the light.",
  "ideasAnalogousTrioStep5": "Add the dark of the middle colour only in the deepest places, and leave the rest in the three.",
  "ideasTriadTitle": "A triad",
  "ideasTriadSummary": "Three colours equally spaced on the wheel; one leads and two support.",
  "ideasTriadPractice": "Using a three-colour scheme with one colour in charge.",
  "ideasTriadSubject": "A poster, a book cover or a big flower with small details.",
  "ideasTriadTip": "Keep the three colours apart on the paper. One grey over all of them ties the scheme together.",
  "ideasTriadStep1": "Decide which of the three colours leads: it gets most of the paper.",
  "ideasTriadStep2": "Colour the main areas with the leading colour.",
  "ideasTriadStep3": "Use the second colour on about a quarter of the drawing, and never let it touch the first while it is wet.",
  "ideasTriadStep4": "Use the third colour in a few small spots only.",
  "ideasTriadStep5": "Shade all three colours with the same grey so they belong together.",
  "ideasSplitComplementTitle": "Split Complementary",
  "ideasSplitComplementSummary": "One colour family and the two colours on either side of its opposite.",
  "ideasSplitComplementPractice": "Balancing a main family with two colours close to its opposite.",
  "ideasSplitComplementSubject": "A vase with two flowers, or a cup with two small objects beside it.",
  "ideasSplitComplementTip": "A near-opposite reads as a clash or a harmony depending on how much of it you use.",
  "ideasSplitComplementStep1": "Draw a subject with one big area and two small ones, such as a vase with two flowers.",
  "ideasSplitComplementStep2": "Cover the big area with the light tone of the main family.",
  "ideasSplitComplementStep3": "Add its shadow where the light does not reach.",
  "ideasSplitComplementStep4": "Use one of the two opposites on the first small area.",
  "ideasSplitComplementStep5": "Use the other opposite on the second small area and keep the two apart.",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesTitle": "Warm greys, cool greys",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesSummary": "Colour the same stone twice, once with warm greys and once with cool ones.",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesPractice": "Telling warm grey from cool grey by eye.",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesSubject": "The same pebble or a folded cloth, drawn twice side by side.",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesTip": "Most brands sell warm, cool and neutral greys as separate families. Pick one family for each half.",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesStep1": "Pencil the same pebble twice, side by side, and mark the light on each.",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesStep2": "First pebble: the light warm grey all over.",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesStep3": "Add the darker warm grey on the shadow side and darken the contact with the ground.",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesStep4": "Second pebble: the light cool grey all over.",
  "ideasTwoGrayTemperaturesStep5": "Add the darker cool grey the same way, then compare: one pebble sits in the sun, the other in the shade.",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentTitle": "Grey with one accent",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentSummary": "Everything in grey except one small thing in colour.",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentPractice": "Making one colour carry the whole drawing.",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentSubject": "A street with a red door, or a yellow umbrella in a grey town.",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentTip": "The accent wants to be as strong as it can be: two layers of the same marker.",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentStep1": "Draw a scene with one object you want the eye to land on, such as a red door in a street.",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentStep2": "Cover everything but that object with the light grey.",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentStep3": "Put the mid grey in the shadows, as if the scene were a photograph.",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentStep4": "Use the darkest grey for the windows, under the eaves and the contact lines.",
  "ideasGrayPlusAccentStep5": "Colour the object alone, with two layers so the accent is as strong as it can be.",
  "ideasEarthTonesTitle": "Earth tones",
  "ideasEarthTonesSummary": "Browns, ochres and an olive green, nothing bright.",
  "ideasEarthTonesPractice": "Layering low-chroma colours without them going dull.",
  "ideasEarthTonesSubject": "Bread, a basket or a ploughed field.",
  "ideasEarthTonesTip": "Low-chroma colours layer well because the paper keeps lighting them from underneath.",
  "ideasEarthTonesStep1": "Draw something that came from the land, such as bread, a basket or a ploughed field.",
  "ideasEarthTonesStep2": "Lay the pale ochre over everything as the first layer.",
  "ideasEarthTonesStep3": "Add the brown where the form turns away from the light.",
  "ideasEarthTonesStep4": "Put the olive green on anything green, and let the ochre under it mute it.",
  "ideasEarthTonesStep5": "Keep the darkest brown for the deepest places only.",
  "ideasJewelTonesTitle": "Jewel tones",
  "ideasJewelTonesSummary": "Deep, saturated colours with a gold accent, like a velvet box.",
  "ideasJewelTonesPractice": "Painting dark saturated colours that keep their shine.",
  "ideasJewelTonesSubject": "A few gems or bottles with a highlight planned on each.",
  "ideasJewelTonesTip": "Dark saturated ink stays glossy on marker paper. A white gel dot on top gives the glass look.",
  "ideasJewelTonesStep1": "Draw a few simple gems or bottles and plan a highlight on each one.",
  "ideasJewelTonesStep2": "Cover the biggest shape with the deep blue-green, in two layers.",
  "ideasJewelTonesStep3": "Use the deep violet on the next shape.",
  "ideasJewelTonesStep4": "Use the deep red on the smallest one.",
  "ideasJewelTonesStep5": "Add the gold yellow to the settings or the caps, then a hard white gel dot on every highlight.",
  "ideasLowKeyTitle": "Dark markers only",
  "ideasLowKeySummary": "Draw with markers darker than mid grey and let a few bits of paper carry all the light.",
  "ideasLowKeyPractice": "Painting a dark picture with light left out on purpose.",
  "ideasLowKeySubject": "A cat with lit eyes, a lamp in a dark room or a profile.",
  "ideasLowKeyTip": "Dark alcohol ink covers evenly in one pass, so the white shapes you leave stay clean.",
  "ideasLowKeyStep1": "Plan the drawing as a few shapes of paper left white on a dark field: a cat's eyes, a lamp, a profile.",
  "ideasLowKeyStep2": "Cover everything except the white shapes with the least dark marker.",
  "ideasLowKeyStep3": "Use the middle marker on the bigger shadows.",
  "ideasLowKeyStep4": "Put the darkest marker on the edges of the light shapes, so they glow by contrast.",
  "ideasLowKeyStep5": "Add white gel on one or two points only, no more.",
  "ideasComplementGradientTitle": "Through the middle",
  "ideasComplementGradientSummary": "Blend two opposite colours into each other and see the grey they make where they meet.",
  "ideasComplementGradientPractice": "Mixing two colours on the paper and reading the result.",
  "ideasComplementGradientSubject": "A long strip of paper with the two colours meeting in the middle.",
  "ideasComplementGradientTip": "Wet opposites mix on the paper at once. Keep both damp and let them meet in the middle.",
  "ideasComplementGradientStep1": "Tape a long strip of paper down.",
  "ideasComplementGradientStep2": "Fill the left third and fade the colour out towards the middle.",
  "ideasComplementGradientStep3": "Fill the right third and fade it into the middle while the first colour is still damp.",
  "ideasComplementGradientStep4": "Compare the muddy middle with a stroke of your grey next to it.",
  "ideasComplementGradientStep5": "Redo the strip with the grey laid in the middle first and the two colours blended into it: a cleaner way through.",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowTitle": "Warm light, cool shadow",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowSummary": "One object, lit side in warm colours and shadow side in cool ones.",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowPractice": "Glazing warm and cool over one base colour.",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowSubject": "A round fruit, an egg or a ball.",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowTip": "Leave a thin band of the plain base between the warm and the cool: it keeps the form turning.",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowStep1": "Draw a round object and mark where the light comes from.",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowStep2": "Cover the whole object with its own colour.",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowStep3": "Glaze the warm colour over the lit half and blend it into the base while it is wet.",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowStep4": "Glaze the cool colour over the shadow half, and leave a thin band of plain base between the two.",
  "ideasWarmLightCoolShadowStep5": "Keep the darkest marker for the contact with the ground only.",
  "ideasVividAndMutedTitle": "Vivid and muted",
  "ideasVividAndMutedSummary": "Colour the same flower with a bright pair and then a muted pair of the same hue.",
  "ideasVividAndMutedPractice": "Telling a vivid colour from its dusty version.",
  "ideasVividAndMutedSubject": "The same flower drawn twice, side by side.",
  "ideasVividAndMutedTip": "Most ranges carry a vivid and a dusty version of each hue. Choosing between them is the whole exercise.",
  "ideasVividAndMutedStep1": "Pencil the same flower twice, side by side.",
  "ideasVividAndMutedStep2": "First flower: the vivid light tone.",
  "ideasVividAndMutedStep3": "Add the vivid shadow of the same hue.",
  "ideasVividAndMutedStep4": "Second flower: the muted light tone of the same hue.",
  "ideasVividAndMutedStep5": "Add its muted shadow, then decide which flower would go in the foreground of a drawing and which in the background.",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderTitle": "One marker and the blender",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderSummary": "Four values out of one dark marker by diluting it with the colourless blender.",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderPractice": "Diluting ink to get lighter values without another marker.",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderSubject": "Four small squares of paper taped side by side.",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderTip": "The blender is pure solvent: it turns a single dark marker into its own light family.",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderStep1": "Tape four small squares of paper side by side.",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderStep2": "Fill the first square with the marker at full strength.",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderStep3": "Second square: one stroke of marker, then the blender over it while it is wet.",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderStep4": "Third square: blender first, then marker into the wet blender. Fourth: touch the marker to the blender nib and paint with the blender only.",
  "ideasOneMarkerBlenderStep5": "Line them up: a four-step ladder out of a single marker.",
  "ideasColourWheelTitle": "Your colour wheel",
  "ideasColourWheelSummary": "Lay out twelve of your markers as a wheel and find the gaps in your collection.",
  "ideasColourWheelPractice": "Reading a colour wheel with the markers you own.",
  "ideasColourWheelSubject": "A circle about ten centimetres across, divided into twelve wedges.",
  "ideasColourWheelTip": "A marker wheel is a shopping list and a mixing guide on one page.",
  "ideasColourWheelStep1": "Trace a circle round a cup and divide it into twelve wedges.",
  "ideasColourWheelStep2": "Pick the marker closest to pure yellow, then to red and then to blue, and fill their wedges, four wedges apart.",
  "ideasColourWheelStep3": "Fill the wedges between with the closest markers you own: orange, green, violet, then the six in-betweens.",
  "ideasColourWheelStep4": "Write each code under its wedge. An empty or doubtful wedge is a marker you might want next.",
  "ideasDeepSkinTitle": "Deep skin tones",
  "ideasDeepSkinSummary": "Colour a face in deep skin tones, with a warm light and a reddish blush.",
  "ideasDeepSkinPractice": "Shading deep skin with a long range of one hue.",
  "ideasDeepSkinSubject": "A face in three-quarter view, lit from the front.",
  "ideasDeepSkinTip": "Deep skin needs a long lightness range in one hue, which marker families are built for.",
  "ideasDeepSkinStep1": "Pencil a face and mark the forehead, the nose and the cheekbones as the lit planes.",
  "ideasDeepSkinStep2": "Cover the whole face with the lightest brown.",
  "ideasDeepSkinStep3": "While the first layer is damp, add the red-brown on the cheeks and the lips.",
  "ideasDeepSkinStep4": "Add the base brown everywhere except on the lit planes.",
  "ideasDeepSkinStep5": "Use the darkest marker under the brows, the nose, the lower lip and the jaw, and leave the lit planes with only the first layer.",
  "ideasBlackObjectTitle": "Black without black",
  "ideasBlackObjectSummary": "Colour a black cat or boot with dark blues and violets, saving the black marker for last.",
  "ideasBlackObjectPractice": "Building a dark object out of dark colours instead of black.",
  "ideasBlackObjectSubject": "A black cat, a boot or a shiny kettle.",
  "ideasBlackObjectTip": "A flat black marker kills the form. Layered dark colours keep it.",
  "ideasBlackObjectStep1": "Pencil the object and the shape of its reflections: black things mirror the sky and the room.",
  "ideasBlackObjectStep2": "Use the pale cool grey-blue on the reflections and the top planes.",
  "ideasBlackObjectStep3": "Cover most of the object with the dark blue.",
  "ideasBlackObjectStep4": "Use the dark violet on the underside.",
  "ideasBlackObjectStep5": "Keep the darkest marker for the deepest creases, and add a white gel glint if the surface is shiny.",
  "ideasWhiteObjectTitle": "White without white",
  "ideasWhiteObjectSummary": "A white cup or egg with pale greys, one tinted shadow and plenty of untouched paper.",
  "ideasWhiteObjectPractice": "Painting a white object without painting over the paper.",
  "ideasWhiteObjectSubject": "A white cup, an egg or a folded napkin.",
  "ideasWhiteObjectTip": "A white object is mostly paper. Markers reward leaving it alone.",
  "ideasWhiteObjectStep1": "Pencil the object and mark the lit side: that side stays paper.",
  "ideasWhiteObjectStep2": "Use the very pale tinted marker on the shadow side only.",
  "ideasWhiteObjectStep3": "Put the light grey inside that, towards the darkest part.",
  "ideasWhiteObjectStep4": "Use the slightly darker cool grey only along the edge furthest from the light and where the object meets the table.",
  "ideasWhiteObjectStep5": "Add a hint of the colour of the table reflected into the underside of the object.",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgeTitle": "Feathered edge",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgeSummary": "Make one side of a shape fade into white paper and keep the other side crisp.",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgePractice": "A soft edge next to a hard one: the simplest way to give a shape light.",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgeSubject": "A leaf, a petal or a fold of cloth.",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgeTip": "Lift the nib as you go: the ink runs out of the stroke on its own.",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgeStep1": "Pencil a leaf and decide which edge is in the light.",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgeStep2": "Colour the dark edge with the base in short strokes that all point towards the light side.",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgeStep3": "Pull the light marker from the middle out over the ends of those strokes, lifting the nib as you go so each stroke dies out.",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgeStep4": "Go over the join again, always moving from colour into white.",
  "ideasFeatheredEdgeStep5": "Compare the two edges: one soft, one hard. That contrast is what makes the shape read.",
  "ideasTipToTipTitle": "Tip to tip",
  "ideasTipToTipSummary": "Load a dark colour onto a light marker nib and lay a gradient in one pass.",
  "ideasTipToTipPractice": "Borrowing ink from one marker to another for a quick gradient.",
  "ideasTipToTipSubject": "A strip of paper the width of two fingers.",
  "ideasTipToTipTip": "Hold the dark marker still: the light nib only takes ink from the tip.",
  "ideasTipToTipStep1": "Tape a strip about the width of two fingers.",
  "ideasTipToTipStep2": "Hold the dark marker still and touch the light marker nib to it for a couple of seconds.",
  "ideasTipToTipStep3": "Start at the bottom of the strip with the loaded nib: the first strokes are dark, then the colour of the marker comes back.",
  "ideasTipToTipStep4": "Scribble the nib on a scrap until it runs clean again: the light marker is not spoilt.",
  "ideasTipToTipStep5": "Repeat on a second strip, loading the nib for longer: the dark part reaches further.",
  "ideasBlenderPatternTitle": "Blender patterns",
  "ideasBlenderPatternSummary": "Lift dots and lines out of a flat colour with the colourless blender.",
  "ideasBlenderPatternPractice": "Moving ink instead of erasing it: dots, lines and patterns.",
  "ideasBlenderPatternSubject": "A square of colour to draw the pattern on.",
  "ideasBlenderPatternTip": "Wait between touches: two marks too close run into one.",
  "ideasBlenderPatternStep1": "Fill a square with the base and let it dry fully.",
  "ideasBlenderPatternStep2": "Press the fine point of the blender into the colour and hold for a second: a pale dot opens with a darker rim.",
  "ideasBlenderPatternStep3": "Draw a line with the broad nib of the blender: the pushed ink gathers at the edges.",
  "ideasBlenderPatternStep4": "Make a pattern: polka dots, stripes or a row of fish scales.",
  "ideasBlenderPatternStep5": "Wait between the touches or the dots run together.",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersTitle": "One marker, three layers",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersSummary": "Shade a cube with a single marker by layering one, two and three passes.",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersPractice": "How much range one marker has when the layers dry one on top of the other.",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersSubject": "A cube with the light coming from one top corner.",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersTip": "Let every layer dry: a wet second pass moves the ink instead of darkening it.",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersStep1": "Pencil a cube with the light coming from one top corner.",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersStep2": "One pass on every face. Let it dry.",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersStep3": "A second pass on the two faces away from the light.",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersStep4": "A third pass only on the darkest face and along the bottom edges.",
  "ideasOneMarkerLayersStep5": "Look at the three values: that is how much range a single marker has.",
  "ideasStipplePeelTitle": "Stippled orange peel",
  "ideasStipplePeelSummary": "Build the dimpled texture of a citrus peel with dots over a blend.",
  "ideasStipplePeelPractice": "Stippling on top of a dry blend, without smearing it.",
  "ideasStipplePeelSubject": "An orange with a soft highlight on its upper side.",
  "ideasStipplePeelTip": "Use the fine point of the nib: it gives clean specks.",
  "ideasStipplePeelStep1": "Pencil an orange and a soft highlight on its upper side.",
  "ideasStipplePeelStep2": "Fill the orange, keeping the highlight paler.",
  "ideasStipplePeelStep3": "Blend the base into the shadow half while it is wet.",
  "ideasStipplePeelStep4": "Once dry, tap the fine point of the shadow marker in loose dots, closer together in the shadow and almost none near the highlight.",
  "ideasStipplePeelStep5": "Tap a few light dots inside the shadow dots to break them up.",
  "ideasClearGlassTitle": "A clear glass",
  "ideasClearGlassSummary": "Colour an empty drinking glass with greys and almost nothing else.",
  "ideasClearGlassPractice": "Grey and white paper: the lightest way to draw glass.",
  "ideasClearGlassSubject": "A tumbler with the rim, the foot and two vertical highlights.",
  "ideasClearGlassTip": "Glass is darkest where it is thickest: the rim and the foot.",
  "ideasClearGlassStep1": "Pencil the glass and mark the rim, the base and two vertical highlights that stay white paper.",
  "ideasClearGlassStep2": "Colour the glass with the lightest grey, leaving the highlights and most of the middle white.",
  "ideasClearGlassStep3": "Darken the rim, the thick base and the far edge: glass is darkest where it is thickest.",
  "ideasClearGlassStep4": "Lay a patch of colour behind the glass and let a paler version of it show through the glass, over the light grey.",
  "ideasClearGlassStep5": "White gel on the rim and the base edge for the sharpest glints.",
  "ideasPolishedMetalTitle": "Polished metal",
  "ideasPolishedMetalSummary": "Make a spoon look like metal with hard bands of grey and one reflected colour.",
  "ideasPolishedMetalPractice": "Hard-edged bands and a long range of values: how metal reads.",
  "ideasPolishedMetalSubject": "A spoon with the bands of its reflection drawn in.",
  "ideasPolishedMetalTip": "Metal has no soft transitions: keep the edges of the bands crisp.",
  "ideasPolishedMetalStep1": "Pencil the spoon and draw its reflections as bands: a dark one across the bowl, a light one next to it, no soft transitions.",
  "ideasPolishedMetalStep2": "Fill the bowl and the handle with the lightest grey.",
  "ideasPolishedMetalStep3": "Lay the dark bands with crisp edges, following the curve of the bowl.",
  "ideasPolishedMetalStep4": "Put the darkest grey right next to the brightest band: metal lives on that contrast.",
  "ideasPolishedMetalStep5": "Let a smudge of the colour of the table reflect in the lower half of the bowl, then white gel on the sharpest glint.",
  "ideasCastShadowTitle": "The shadow on the table",
  "ideasCastShadowSummary": "Draw a cup and get its cast shadow right: dark at the contact, soft further away.",
  "ideasCastShadowPractice": "Cast shadows: the contact line, the fade and the grey that draws them.",
  "ideasCastShadowSubject": "A cup on a table, with the light from one side.",
  "ideasCastShadowTip": "The shadow starts where the object touches the table, not where the light ends.",
  "ideasCastShadowStep1": "Pencil a cup and the shape of its shadow on the table, longer on the side away from the light.",
  "ideasCastShadowStep2": "Colour the cup; shade its far side with the same marker in a second layer.",
  "ideasCastShadowStep3": "Fill the cast shadow with the grey, starting at the base of the cup and lifting as you move away.",
  "ideasCastShadowStep4": "Add a thin dark line where the cup touches the table.",
  "ideasCastShadowStep5": "Soften the far edge of the shadow with a second light touch of grey while the first is still damp.",
  "ideasSoftCloudsTitle": "Soft clouds",
  "ideasSoftCloudsSummary": "Colour the sky around the clouds and leave the clouds as paper.",
  "ideasSoftCloudsPractice": "Negative painting: the shape is the paper you did not touch.",
  "ideasSoftCloudsSubject": "Two or three clouds with round tops and flat bottoms.",
  "ideasSoftCloudsTip": "Work around the clouds with the broad nib: their edges keep a ragged look.",
  "ideasSoftCloudsStep1": "Pencil two or three cloud shapes very lightly: round tops, flatter bottoms.",
  "ideasSoftCloudsStep2": "Fill the sky with the light blue, working around the clouds with the broad nib so their edges stay a bit ragged.",
  "ideasSoftCloudsStep3": "Darken the sky towards the top and blend down into the light blue while it is wet.",
  "ideasSoftCloudsStep4": "Shade the underside of each cloud with the light grey, keeping the tops white.",
  "ideasSoftCloudsStep5": "Soften any hard sky edge against a cloud with the blender, pushing the colour outwards.",
  "ideasStillWaterTitle": "Reflection in still water",
  "ideasStillWaterSummary": "Draw a tree on a bank and its upside-down, slightly darker copy in the water.",
  "ideasStillWaterPractice": "Glazing a colour over another one: how a reflection is made.",
  "ideasStillWaterSubject": "A tree on a bank with its reflection in the water below.",
  "ideasStillWaterTip": "Paint the reflection in horizontal strokes only: the water moves sideways.",
  "ideasStillWaterStep1": "Pencil a horizon line, a simple tree above it and its mirror image below.",
  "ideasStillWaterStep2": "Colour the tree; colour its reflection with the same marker in horizontal strokes only.",
  "ideasStillWaterStep3": "Lay the light blue over the whole water area, including the reflection, in long horizontal strokes.",
  "ideasStillWaterStep4": "Darken the water near the bank and the far edge; a few broken horizontal lines of base across the reflection.",
  "ideasStillWaterStep5": "Put the dark blue where the bank meets the water, then two or three thin white gel lines for ripples.",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonTitle": "Twisted ribbon",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonSummary": "Blend a gradient that bends with a ribbon as it twists over and under itself.",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonPractice": "Repeating a short blend on every turn of a shape.",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonSubject": "A ribbon with two twists, over and under itself.",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonTip": "Blend along the curve of the ribbon, never straight across it.",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonStep1": "Pencil a ribbon with two twists; mark which segments show the front and which the back.",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonStep2": "Fill every segment with the light marker.",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonStep3": "On each segment, add the base on the side that curves away from you, in strokes that follow the curve and not straight across.",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonStep4": "Darken only where a segment passes under another, as a narrow band right at the overlap.",
  "ideasTwistedRibbonStep5": "Blend with the blender along each band where the gradient looks stepped.",
  "ideasNoLineLeafTitle": "No-line colouring",
  "ideasNoLineLeafSummary": "Colour a leaf from a pencil sketch and let the edges of the colour do the work of the outline.",
  "ideasNoLineLeafPractice": "Drawing without a liner: the edge of the colour is the outline.",
  "ideasNoLineLeafSubject": "A simple leaf with its central vein.",
  "ideasNoLineLeafTip": "Lift the pencil with a soft eraser until you can only just see it.",
  "ideasNoLineLeafStep1": "Draw the leaf and its central vein very lightly in pencil, then lift most of the graphite with a soft eraser until you can only just see it.",
  "ideasNoLineLeafStep2": "Fill the leaf; stop exactly at the pencil line.",
  "ideasNoLineLeafStep3": "Shade one half and the base of the leaf while the first layer is damp.",
  "ideasNoLineLeafStep4": "Define the edge on the shadow side with a thin line of the darkest marker instead of a liner.",
  "ideasNoLineLeafStep5": "Erase any pencil still showing once the ink is dry.",
  "ideasShortFurTitle": "Short fur",
  "ideasShortFurSummary": "Colour the ear of a cat with short flicks in two directions.",
  "ideasShortFurPractice": "Short strokes that follow the direction the hair grows.",
  "ideasShortFurSubject": "A cat ear with a few arrows showing the way the fur grows.",
  "ideasShortFurTip": "Flick from the roots outwards: press, move fast and lift.",
  "ideasShortFurStep1": "Pencil the ear and draw the direction the fur grows with a few arrows.",
  "ideasShortFurStep2": "Fill the ear with the light marker, flicking outward at the rim so the edge is furry and not solid.",
  "ideasShortFurStep3": "Short flicks of base from the inner edge outward, following the arrows.",
  "ideasShortFurStep4": "Fewer and darker flicks inside the ear and at the base where it meets the head.",
  "ideasShortFurStep5": "Flick the light marker back over the joins to merge the layers and soften the darkest flicks.",
  "ideasWoodGrainTitle": "Wood plank",
  "ideasWoodGrainSummary": "A plank with grain lines and a knot, in three browns.",
  "ideasWoodGrainPractice": "Long strokes that follow the shape of the material.",
  "ideasWoodGrainSubject": "A wooden plank with a knot near one end.",
  "ideasWoodGrainTip": "The grain flows around the knot: draw the lines bending, never straight.",
  "ideasWoodGrainStep1": "Pencil a plank and a knot; the grain flows around the knot like water around a stone.",
  "ideasWoodGrainStep2": "Fill the plank with long strokes along the grain.",
  "ideasWoodGrainStep3": "Long, slightly wavy lines of base along the grain, bunching up near the knot.",
  "ideasWoodGrainStep4": "A few darker lines and the rings of the knot; darken the lower edge of the plank.",
  "ideasWoodGrainStep5": "Go back over the lines with the light marker to soften where they look drawn on.",
  "ideasBrickWallTitle": "Brick wall",
  "ideasBrickWallSummary": "A patch of brick wall with bricks of slightly different colours and pale mortar.",
  "ideasBrickWallPractice": "Flat fills, one brick at a time, and the grey of the mortar.",
  "ideasBrickWallSubject": "A patch of wall with about twenty bricks.",
  "ideasBrickWallTip": "Leave the mortar as paper: it is lighter than any brick.",
  "ideasBrickWallStep1": "Pencil the mortar grid; leave it as paper for now.",
  "ideasBrickWallStep2": "Fill every brick with the light marker, letting each brick dry as a separate shape.",
  "ideasBrickWallStep3": "Go over about half the bricks with the base, chosen at random, so the wall is not uniform.",
  "ideasBrickWallStep4": "Shadow on the lower edge of each brick, plus a few chips and some darker bricks.",
  "ideasBrickWallStep5": "Fill the mortar lines with the light grey.",
  "ideasFoldedClothTitle": "Folded cloth",
  "ideasFoldedClothSummary": "Shade the folds of a hanging cloth with four values and one direction of light.",
  "ideasFoldedClothPractice": "The same gradient repeated on every fold.",
  "ideasFoldedClothSubject": "A cloth hanging over a line, with three or four folds.",
  "ideasFoldedClothTip": "The darkest line goes only where the cloth turns under itself.",
  "ideasFoldedClothStep1": "Pencil a cloth draped over a line, three or four folds; mark the top of each fold.",
  "ideasFoldedClothStep2": "Fill the cloth.",
  "ideasFoldedClothStep3": "Base on the side of each fold away from the light, blended into the light while it is damp.",
  "ideasFoldedClothStep4": "Shadow deep inside each fold, where the cloth turns under.",
  "ideasFoldedClothStep5": "A thin dark line only at the very bottom of the deepest folds; blend with the blender any hard step on the round parts.",
  "ideasWaterDropsTitle": "Drops on a leaf",
  "ideasWaterDropsSummary": "Three water drops on a leaf, each with a shadow, a dark edge and a bright glint.",
  "ideasWaterDropsPractice": "A few crisp marks on top of a finished blend.",
  "ideasWaterDropsSubject": "A leaf with three drops of water on it.",
  "ideasWaterDropsTip": "The drop reverses the light: shade the side that faces it.",
  "ideasWaterDropsStep1": "Pencil a leaf and three drops; mark in each drop a small highlight on the light side.",
  "ideasWaterDropsStep2": "Colour the whole leaf, drops included; the drops are the leaf seen through water.",
  "ideasWaterDropsStep3": "Shade the leaf, and inside each drop shade the side towards the light, because the drop acts as a lens and reverses it.",
  "ideasWaterDropsStep4": "A thin dark crescent on the far side of each drop and a small cast shadow under it.",
  "ideasWaterDropsStep5": "White gel: one hard dot per drop on the light side, plus a faint lighter rim opposite it.",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsTitle": "Strawberry",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsSummary": "A strawberry whose seeds sit in small dimples, with a green cap.",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsPractice": "Texture made of small marks: dimples, seeds and a cap.",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsSubject": "A strawberry with a loose grid of seeds.",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsTip": "Tap the dimples while the colour is still damp: the darker ring forms by itself.",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsStep1": "Pencil the strawberry and a loose grid of seed dots, closer together towards the tip.",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsStep2": "Fill the fruit, leaving a soft highlight.",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsStep3": "Blend the base around the sides; while damp, press a slightly darker dot above each seed position.",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsStep4": "Darken the lower half and the dimple above each seed.",
  "ideasStrawberrySeedsStep5": "Colour the leaves and the stalk with the green, then a tiny pale gel dot in each dimple for the seed.",
  "ideasPaletteMixingTitle": "Mixing on a palette",
  "ideasPaletteMixingSummary": "Make a third colour by mixing two markers on a plastic surface and picking it up with the blender.",
  "ideasPaletteMixingPractice": "Mixing two inks outside the paper, with the blender as the brush.",
  "ideasPaletteMixingSubject": "A plastic lid or a blending card, plus a scrap of paper.",
  "ideasPaletteMixingTip": "The blender only carries what is wet: do not let the puddles dry.",
  "ideasPaletteMixingStep1": "Scribble the first marker onto a non-porous scrap, such as a blending card, a plastic lid or a zip bag.",
  "ideasPaletteMixingStep2": "Scribble the second marker next to it so the two puddles touch.",
  "ideasPaletteMixingStep3": "Pick the mixture up with the broad nib of the colourless blender and paint a swatch with it; next to it, swatch each marker alone.",
  "ideasPaletteMixingStep4": "Try a second mix with more of one of the two colours.",
  "ideasPaletteMixingStep5": "Note which mixes you would like to own as a marker.",
  "ideasLongHairTitle": "Long hair",
  "ideasLongHairSummary": "A fall of hair with a band of light across it and strands that follow the head.",
  "ideasLongHairPractice": "Long tapered strokes and a band of light that is only paper.",
  "ideasLongHairSubject": "A head of hair with a few big locks and a band of light.",
  "ideasLongHairTip": "Press less inside the band of light: the paper does the work.",
  "ideasLongHairStep1": "Pencil the hair as a few big locks and not as single hairs, and a curved band where the light hits.",
  "ideasLongHairStep2": "Fill all the hair, pressing less inside the light band.",
  "ideasLongHairStep3": "Long strokes from the parting down, each lifting as it reaches the light band, then from the ends up towards it.",
  "ideasLongHairStep4": "Darken between the locks and under the ear and the neck.",
  "ideasLongHairStep5": "A few thin strands of the darkest marker, then two or three fine white gel strands only inside the light band.",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesTitle": "Overlapping glazes",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesSummary": "Overlap three transparent circles and see which new colours appear.",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesPractice": "Transparent layers: every overlap is a real mix.",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesSubject": "Three overlapping circles, like a Venn diagram.",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesTip": "The order matters: the lighter colour on top always looks different.",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesStep1": "Pencil three circles that overlap like a Venn diagram.",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesStep2": "Fill the first circle and let it dry completely.",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesStep3": "Fill the second, including the overlap; the overlap turns a third colour.",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesStep4": "Fill the third circle; the middle, where all three meet, is the darkest mix.",
  "ideasOverlapGlazesStep5": "Repeat with the markers in another order: the lighter one on top always looks different.",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundTitle": "Faded background",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundSummary": "Give a drawing a halo of colour that fades to white long before the edge of the paper.",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundPractice": "A vignette made with circular strokes and the blender.",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundSubject": "A mug, a bird or any small subject in the middle of the page.",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundTip": "Stop before you reach the edge of the paper: the halo fades on its own.",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundStep1": "Pencil a simple subject, such as a mug or a bird, in the middle of the page.",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundStep2": "Colour a ring of base around the subject, about a finger wide, in circular strokes.",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundStep3": "Pull the light marker outwards from the ring in overlapping circles until it runs pale.",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundStep4": "Follow with the blender beyond the colour, pushing the last of it into the paper so there is no edge at all.",
  "ideasFadedBackgroundStep5": "Finish the subject so it is darker than the halo.",
  "ideasHatchShadingTitle": "Hatched shading",
  "ideasHatchShadingSummary": "Shade an apple with lines only, no blending: hatching for the mid-tone and cross-hatching for the shadow.",
  "ideasHatchShadingPractice": "Hatching and cross-hatching: lines that read as a tone.",
  "ideasHatchShadingSubject": "An apple with the highlight marked in.",
  "ideasHatchShadingTip": "Keep the lines parallel and evenly spaced: the eye does the rest.",
  "ideasHatchShadingStep1": "Pencil an apple and mark the highlight.",
  "ideasHatchShadingStep2": "Fill the apple flat and let it dry.",
  "ideasHatchShadingStep3": "Hatch the shadow half with parallel lines of the fine nib, closer together as they get further from the light.",
  "ideasHatchShadingStep4": "Cross the darkest area with a second set of lines at an angle, and add the contact shadow under the apple.",
  "ideasHatchShadingStep5": "Step back: from a distance the lines turn into a shade.",
